###  http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#liberal_privilege_concept
:liberal_privilege_concept rdf:type :BackgroundKnowledge ;
                rdfs:label "Liberal Privilege Concept"@en ;
                rdfs:comment "A social and political concept referring to the perceived advantages or protections that individuals may experience due to their alignment with liberal ideologies, often in contrast to conservative or right-wing perspectives. In this meme, it is weaponized to imply that being 'liberal' (and by extension, being Black) granted the subject immunity from legal consequences, which is a false equivalence and a harmful framing."@en ;
                :extractionMethod "llm_extraction_huggingface" ;
                :extractedFrom "23705.png" ;
                :extractionTimestamp "2025-12-15T18:46:24.889109" .

###  http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#Jussie_Smollett_identity
:Jussie_Smollett_identity rdf:type :BackgroundKnowledge ;
                rdfs:label "Jussie Smollett's Identity"@en ;
                rdfs:comment "Jussie Smollett is a Black American actor, best known for his role as Jamal on the TV show 'Empire'. His identity as a Black man and a liberal (in political alignment) is central to the meme's message. The meme uses his public persona and the assumption of his privilege to make a cynical and harmful claim about systemic justice."@en ;
                :extractionMethod "llm_extraction_huggingface" ;
                :extractedFrom "23705.png" ;
                :extractionTimestamp "2025-12-15T18:46:24.889109" .

###  http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#anti_black_criminality_stereotype
:anti_black_criminality_stereotype rdf:type :BackgroundKnowledge ;
                rdfs:label "Anti-Black Criminality Stereotype"@en ;
                rdfs:comment "A pervasive racial stereotype that associates Black people with criminal behavior, often used to justify systemic racism and discriminatory practices. The meme implicitly invokes this stereotype by suggesting that Blackness (and liberal privilege) led to legal impunity, which reinforces the idea that Black people are inherently more likely to be 'protected' from justice, a harmful and false narrative."@en ;
                :extractionMethod "llm_extraction_huggingface" ;
                :extractedFrom "23705.png" ;
                :extractionTimestamp "2025-12-15T18:46:24.889109" .

###  http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#systemic_justice_bias
:systemic_justice_bias rdf:type :BackgroundKnowledge ;
                rdfs:label "Systemic Justice Bias"@en ;
                rdfs:comment "The concept that the legal system is not impartial and often operates in ways that disadvantage certain racial, ethnic, or socioeconomic groups. The meme references this concept by implying that liberal privilege (and race) shielded the subject from punishment, which is a cynical commentary on systemic bias, though the framing is harmful and misleading."@en ;
                :extractionMethod "llm_extraction_huggingface" ;
                :extractedFrom "23705.png" ;
                :extractionTimestamp "2025-12-15T18:46:24.889109" .

###  http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#legal_impunity
:legal_impunity rdf:type :BackgroundKnowledge ;
                rdfs:label "Legal Impunity"@en ;
                rdfs:comment "The state of being free from legal consequences or punishment, often due to privilege, influence, or systemic bias. The meme uses the phrase 'kept me out of prison' to imply that the subject enjoyed legal impunity, which is a real-world concept that the meme misrepresents by attributing it to 'liberal privilege' rather than systemic factors."@en ;
                :extractionMethod "llm_extraction_huggingface" ;
                :extractedFrom "23705.png" ;
                :extractionTimestamp "2025-12-15T18:46:24.889109" .

###  http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#mocking_smile
:mocking_smile rdf:type :Emotion ;
                rdfs:label "mocking or sarcastic expression"@en ;
                rdfs:comment "The subject, Jussie Smollett, is smiling slightly, which in the context of the meme's text ('my liberal privilege... kept me out of prison') is interpreted as a sarcastic or mocking expression. The smile does not convey genuine happiness but rather a self-aware or ironic tone, aligning with the meme's cynical framing of privilege and impunity."@en ;
                :extractionMethod "llm_extraction_huggingface" ;
                :extractedFrom "23705.png" ;
                :extractionTimestamp "2025-12-15T18:46:24.889109" .

###  http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#liberal_privilege_mapping
:liberal_privilege_mapping rdf:type :AnalogicalMapping ;
                rdfs:label "Jussie Smollett is mapped as the beneficiary of 'liberal privilege'"@en ;
                rdfs:comment "The text 'my liberal privilege liberal privilege kept me out of prison' explicitly attributes the concept of 'liberal privilege' to the person in the image (Jussie Smollett). The visual material (Jussie_Smollett) is mapped onto the textual concept 'liberal privilege' as the entity that possesses or benefits from it. This mapping is central to the meme's harmful intent, as it frames the subject's identity (Black, liberal) as the source of legal impunity, which is a false equivalence and a harmful stereotype."@en ;
                :extractionMethod "llm_extraction_huggingface" ;
                :extractedFrom "23705.png" ;
                :extractionTimestamp "2025-12-15T18:46:24.889109" .

###  http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#semiotic_projection_1
:semiotic_projection_1 rdf:type :SemioticProjection ;
                rdfs:label "User projected onto the textual claim of liberal privilege leading to legal impunity"@en ;
                rdfs:comment "The user is projected onto the textual material 'my liberal privilege liberal privilege kept me out of prison', which uses the first-person pronoun 'my' to imply personal ownership and experience. This deictic 'my' directs the viewer to identify with the speaker (the person in the image) and to project their own potential experience or perspective onto the claim that 'liberal privilege' kept them out of prison. The projection is onto the textual material, which frames the subject's identity as the source of legal impunity."@en ;
                :extractionMethod "llm_extraction_huggingface" ;
                :extractedFrom "23705.png" ;
                :extractionTimestamp "2025-12-15T18:46:24.889109" .

